% ==========================================
% 7. DISCUSSION
% ==========================================
\section{Discussion}

\subsection{Fail-Closed vs. Graceful Degradation}
Traditional systems prioritize availability. Under failure, they degrade gracefully—reducing functionality while maintaining operation. This model is inappropriate for privacy-critical systems. Graceful degradation that preserves operation at the cost of privacy is not graceful; it is a privacy violation.

Fail-closed systems prioritize correctness over availability. When a privacy invariant cannot be verified, the system halts. This is not a defect; it is the intended behavior. A halted system cannot violate privacy. An operating system with uncertain invariants can.

% --- FIGURE 2: FAILURE STATE MACHINE ---
\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[->, >=stealth', shorten >=1pt, auto, node distance=3cm, thick]
  \tikzstyle{state} = [circle, draw, minimum size=1.8cm, align=center, fill=gray!10]

  \node[state, fill=green!20] (Normal) {Normal\\Op};
  \node[state, fill=yellow!20] (Degraded) [right of=Normal] {Degraded\\(Safe)};
  \node[state, fill=red!20] (Halt) [below of=Normal] {HALT\\(Secure)};

  \path 
    (Normal) edge node [above] {Net Loss} (Degraded)
    (Degraded) edge node [below] {Net Restore} (Normal)
    (Normal) edge node [left] {TTL Fail} (Halt)
    (Normal) edge node [right] {Watchdog} (Halt)
    (Degraded) edge node [right] {TTL Fail} (Halt);
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{Fail-Closed State Machine. Any critical invariant violation (TTL, Watchdog) forces a transition to the HALT state. Network issues only trigger the Degraded (Safe) state, where no output is emitted but local processing continues.}
\label{fig:states}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Adversarial Timing Analysis (TOCTOU)}
We analyzed the system for Time-of-Check-to-Time-of-Use (TOCTOU) vulnerabilities during the destruction window. An adversary might attempt to read memory *after* the TTL check but *before* zeroization.
However, the `zeroize()` function executes atomically with respect to the memory allocator. The pointer is not freed until `memset` completes. Since the memory is mapped as `private` and `anonymous`, no other process can map it. The only window of vulnerability is a physical cold-boot attack during the 33ms window, which requires physical access and liquid nitrogen, falling outside the standard threat model.

\subsection{Defense in Depth}
The enforcement mechanisms provide defense in depth:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Primary: TTL enforcement loop (10ms)
    \item Secondary: Independent watchdog (100ms)
    \item Tertiary: Forced zeroization on deallocation
    \item Quaternary: Boot verification of empty state
\end{enumerate}

Each layer can fail independently without compromising the overall guarantee, provided at least one layer remains operational.

\subsection{Limitations}
This verification assumes the hardware behaves as specified. We do not verify:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Cold boot attacks on DRAM
    \item Hardware backdoors in sensor modules
    \item Hypervisor-level memory inspection
\end{itemize}

These threats require physical adversary assumptions beyond the software threat model. These threats require hardware-level countermeasures outside the scope of software verification. Consequently, "zero residue" as reported in this paper refers to the absence of recoverable application-layer references; it does not constitute a claim about physical DRAM-level data remanence.

% ==========================================
% 8. SCOPE AND RELATION TO PRIOR WORK
% ==========================================
\section{Scope and Relation to Prior Work}

\subsection{Relationship to Paper 17}
Paper 17 \cite{b17} defines the canonical eight-layer architecture and states architectural irreversibility as a design principle. This paper \textbf{validates}, but does not extend, those claims. We provide runtime evidence that the architecture behaves as specified.

\subsection{What This Paper Does Not Do}
This paper does not:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Introduce new sensing capabilities
    \item Propose new inference models
    \item Modify governance logic
    \item Add ethical or sociological analysis
    \item Claim performance improvements
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Related Work}
Fault injection testing has a long history in systems research \cite{b1, b2}. Watchdog-based enforcement is standard in safety-critical systems \cite{b3}. Memory zeroization techniques derive from secure deletion literature \cite{b4}. Fail-closed semantics are established in safety-critical computing \cite{b5}. Our contribution is applying these techniques specifically to verify privacy-by-architecture claims in edge AI systems.

\subsection{Hardware Enclaves vs. Software Enforcement}
A common critique is that software enforcement is inferior to hardware enclaves like Intel SGX or ARM TrustZone. We deliberately chose software enforcement for three reasons, as detailed in Costan and Devadas \cite{b20}:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Auditability:} Enclave code is often opaque and signed by vendors, violating the "visible privacy" principle (Paper 15).
    \item \textbf{Portability:} Software watchdogs run on commodity IoT hardware (Raspberry Pi, Jetson Nano) without requiring specialized TEE support.
    \item \textbf{Patchability:} Vulnerabilities in enclaves (e.g., Foreshadow, Plundervolt) require firmware updates that are rarely applied in edge deployments.
\end{enumerate}
Our "defense-in-depth" software approach provides verifiable guarantees on untrusted hardware, a critical requirement for low-cost educational deployments.

\subsection{Future Direction: Formal Verification}
While this work relies on runtime assertion (dynamic verification), future work will explore static verification using TLA+ (Temporal Logic of Actions). We aim to model the interaction between the Watchdog ($W$) and Main Process ($M$) as a state machine:
\begin{equation}
\square (\text{State}(M) = \text{Stale} \implies \Diamond \text{State}(M) = \text{Halted})
\end{equation}
Proving this liveness property mathematically would complement the empirical evidence presented here, providing a complete verification stack from logic to runtime.

% ==========================================
% 9. CONCLUSION
% ==========================================
\section{Conclusion}

This paper demonstrates that ScholarMasterEngine's architectural privacy guarantees are enforced at runtime. Through systematic fault injection across 475 failure scenarios, we verified zero application-layer post-crash data residue, 100\% watchdog termination success, and correct system halt behavior. All six paper-local invariants (P18-INV-01 through P18-INV-06)—L3 boundary non-crossing, TTL compliance, restart non-replay, governance non-bypass, watchdog independence, and zeroization completeness—were verified without violation. The system is fail-closed: it halts rather than degrades privacy. This paper provides the enforcement evidence necessary to defend the architectural claims of Paper 17 against hostile verification.

